Pass 2, done on ack-paket 2026-11-16 (skilletor-worker, reviewed by main):

What changed:
- The starter reads the house route. URL: …/releases/download/<version>/skilletor-<version>-<platform>[.exe], with bare tags (tag_format %v). The pin is the release's SHA256SUMS committed as is. The parser skips other binaries, malformed versions and the .exe line on POSIX. The cache is keyed by version.
- `--version` and `-v` never provision. They answer from the engine that runs (cached binary, else Node, else Perl) with its own output: binary and Perl print 0.008, Node prints 0.8.0. The starter adds nothing (ACK c14, hub).
- Perl runs from lib/, script/skilletor and cpanfile at the root.
- SessionStart provisions detached (setsid, else perl setsid(), else a plain background start with HUP ignored) and never waits. The session runs Node or Perl; the next one runs the binary. The hook takes 33-40 ms median instead of 6 s with a slow server. The provision survives a SIGKILL of the hook's process group. A CLI command still provisions in the foreground.
- install.sh's no-Node preflight runs --help (which provisions) instead of --version. In a sandbox the install tests go 16/16 with the change and 12/16 without it.

Decisions:
- Detached provisioning instead of a time bound. Reason: a bound can't guarantee the hook never overruns its budget. SessionStart shares 30 s (Hermes 20 s) with the sync, and the binary's first-run unpack alone takes 10+ s.
- Not added: a stale-.tmp sweep (SIGTERM cleans up, tested) and an in-flight guard (a double download is harmless; the rename is atomic).
- Developer override: still deferred.

Limits:
- perl setsid() is untested on real macOS (bash --posix stood in).
- In a 35-185 ms window after start, a group kill can still catch the provision; the next SessionStart retries. Hermes doesn't kill the group after a normal exit (code read). Claude Code and Codex: not checked.

